The conversation centered on a paper discussing the "blue social cost of carbon" (SCC) and the role of ocean impacts in climate-economy models. The user expressed confusion regarding the concept of substitutability and its relationship to willingness-to-pay (WTP) for valuing ecosystem services. We explored how WTP captures individual value but may not reflect broader societal welfare implications, including externalities and the intrinsic worth of non-use values. The substitutability adjustment helps contextualize these values in assessing overall welfare. The user further questioned why welfare implications aren't simply the sum of everyone's WTP, leading to a discussion about the complexities of individual vs. societal perspectives, distributional inequities, dynamic contexts, and systemic risks. Ultimately, the dialogue reinforced the need for comprehensive assessments in environmental economics that account for broader impacts beyond individual monetary valuations.
